description:
Model how named kernel objects are created, opened and destroyed (lesson 4.01):

```cpp
enum class Kind { Event, Mutex, Semaphore };
enum class Result { Created, Opened, NotFound, WrongType };

class ObjectNamespace
{
public:
    Result create(Kind kind, const std::string& name, int& handle);
    Result open(Kind kind, const std::string& name, int& handle);
    void close(int handle);
    bool exists(const std::string& name) const;
    int handleCount(const std::string& name) const;
};
```

- Handles are numbered 1, 2, 3, ... and never reused. Each successful `create` or `open` returns a new handle to the object.
- **create**: if no object has that name, it makes one and returns `Created`. If an object of the **same kind** exists, it opens it and returns `Opened` (the `ERROR_ALREADY_EXISTS` case). If an object of a **different kind** has the name, `WrongType` and no handle.
- **open**: like create, but returns `NotFound` instead of creating.
- Names are compared **ignoring case**.
- **close** releases a handle; when an object's last handle closes, the object and its name are gone. Closing an unknown or already closed handle does nothing.
- **handleCount** is the number of open handles to the named object (0 if none).

